Subject: Update on my previous comments on choosing a time transform in cox.zph
Message-ID: <1508FB10AFDBD211854D0090273C41070EB90628@com83-178.medicine.uiowa.edu>

Hi, folks:

I need to update and correct some of the conclusions that I posted about a
week ago about the cox.zph function, at least about the version of cox.zph
in SPlus for Windows 6.2.  (I have uninstalled 6.1 and can't check this out
easily in 6.1 or earlier versions.)

There are, of course, two forms of formulas in the coxph function:  

a)  The usual Surv(time, censor) form, and 
b)  The counting process formulation form -- Surv(start,stop,censor).

When one runs cox.zph on a cox object with the usual form (a above), the
default time transform is 'km' just as it says in the documentation.  One
can opt one of the other time transforms by explicitly requesting it in the
function call. I.e.: 
        cox.zph(test.cox,transform='identity')

For historic reasons that are no longer relevant, when one runs cox.zph on a
cox object with a formula of type b) above, the default time transform is
set to 'identity'.   However, if one wants to use another time transform
(e.g., 'km'), one can specify that in the call and the function will now
return correct answers.  

If you have an earlier S-Plus version that doesn't handle the 'km' transform
in counting process models, the cox.zph program will apparently crash and
burn.  So you can probably be assured that if you get an answer in your
version, it will be a correct answer.

Sorry for the somewhat erroneous post earlier.

Larry Hunsicker

