Subject: Is testing for COINTEGRATION the right thing to do for my time series data?
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20040109151842.01e26040@pop3.snl.salk.edu>

Hi,

	I have two two time series vectors, Y1 and Y2, each of which represents 
the level of blood flow to a specific area of the human brain.  The 
measurements were made at 1 minute intervals for 15 minutes, so each vector 
has 15 elements.   Each vector is integrated of order 4.

	Now, I'd like to find out if the blood flow to these two areas of the 
brain follows the same pattern or 'trend'.  That is, does the blood flow 
rise and fall in the same fashion (perhaps with a lag) between the two 
areas?  When the blood flow to one area is high, can the other area be 
expected to have a high flow as well (with or without a +ve or -ve lag)?

	I'm told a good way of testing this is the the Granger and Engle 
cointegration method.  (Actually, using this method, the two time series 
are cointegrated of the order 2, indicating they do follow the same overall 
pattern.)

	My question is, is cointegration the best method for addressing my problem 
or are there better, even more sophisticated methods for doing this?

	Any input would be greatly appreciated.

        Thanks in advance,
