Thanks to Sam 
Buttrey, Simon Rosenfeld, Tony Plate, Rolf Turner, and Yuelin Li for their quick 
responses.  The real underlying problem was that there was an NA in one of 
my vectors that I did not account for.  Once I added !is.na(vec) to the 
logical statment for the 'good' vector, there were no problems.  So the 
moral of the story is to watch out for those elusive NAs in your data.  
Thanks again to everyone.

 

Win

 

