From ent3c at virginia.edu  Wed Jan 14 15:06:00 2004
From: ent3c at virginia.edu (Eric Turkheimer)
Date: Wed, 14 Jan 2004 10:06:00 -0500
Subject: lme: Some answers, more questions
Message-ID: <000401c3daaf$ec1e8160$6501a8c0@Turkheimer>

I posted last week about reproducing the functionality of the GROUP=
option on the RANDOM statement in PROC MIXED in lme.  I didn't hear from
many people, but those who did reply requested a summary of what I
learned.  Here is a partial answer, in the hope of finding someone who
can help me take it further.

The sasMixed package provides one example of a translation of a GROUP=
option into lme, that being output 4.17 on page 163 of the Littell et
al. Mixed Models book, the semiconductor example.  (I changed the
variable names a little) In that example, the code,

PROC MIXED SCORING=4;
CLASS SOURCE LOT WAFER SITE;
MODEL Y=SOURCE/DDFM=SATTERTH;
RANDOM LOT(SOR)/GROUP=SOR;
RANDOM WAFER(SOR LOT);

Can be rendered in lme as:

fmsemi2<-lme(Thickness~Source,data=Semi2,list(Lot = pdDiag( ~ Source - 1
), Wafer = ~ 1 ))

Which produces:

     summary(fmsemi2)
Linear mixed-effects model fit by REML
 Data: Semi2 
       AIC      BIC    logLik 
  457.1494 470.6404 -222.5747

Random effects:
 Formula:  ~ Source - 1 | Lot
 Structure: Diagonal
         Source1  Source2 
StdDev: 4.133049 14.92343

 Formula:  ~ 1 | Wafer %in% Lot
        (Intercept) Residual 
StdDev:    5.988735 3.545333

....

So it does what I wanted, gives separate estimates of the lot effect for
the two levels of source, and I can reproduce the code on my own data.
What I don't understand is why it works.

I think one thing I don't understand is the relation between the
specification of random effects via the usual model statements, as
opposed to named elements in a list.  This lme statement uses both, and
I don't see how they are combined.  I can't find the use of the named
list documented in any detail anywhere.

The second problem is how exactly a random effect of Source across
levels of Lot (note lme's description of the random effect in the
summary) up giving you essentially the random effect of Lot for the two
levels of Source.  Source, after all, isn't really a random factor at
all, is it?  It's just a grouping variable.

So anyway, if someone can help me unpack that lme code, I'd appreciate
it.

Eric

