Subject: Re: nonlinear regression and Excel solver
In-Reply-To: <40057BE5.2060600@uvm.edu>
References: <40057BE5.2060600@uvm.edu>
Message-ID: <000d01c3daca$1c36fac0$8f5a3f92@labor.ak>

Hi,

A few years ago, I tried to use Excel's solver for some numerical work when
I was taking a class in maximum entropy methods.  What I found was that it
was terribly awkward to use (that's rather subjective, of course), and that
it never once managed to find a global optimum for any of the functions I
plugged into it.  Unfortunately, it usually converged, to a wrong answer.
That's not a good thing!

I've since found out that this is part of a pattern: Excel will always give
you a number.  You have to guess whether that number is nonsense or not.
Excel is made to sell, rather than to use.

For over a decade, people like McCullagh have been testing Excel and finding
it wanting, and for that whole time, they've been reporting that the same
old bugs are in each new version.  Look here
http://www.amstat.org/chapters/Alaska/ExcelIssues.html for some recent
links.  The summary seems to be that most folks will get away with using
Excel, most of the time, but it will sometimes screw up your analysis and
will not warn you when it does so.

I wound up using R, a dialect of the S programming language, upon which
S-plus is based.  I was able to use its optim function to do the homework.

I recommend against using Excel for any sort of analysis, but that's based
on facts, like those linked to above, rather than snobbery.  It's just
peachy for data entry, and outputs a usable csv file.  If you are going to
continue within Excel, I'd suggest that you consider using the Poptools
add-in which is mentioned on the page I linked to above.

I'm sorry that I won't be able to offer to give you any significant help,
since my family and job are taking up more time than I have already.

Good luck,
Nels

