From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Wed, 14 Jan 2004 10:52:32 -0800
Subject: Re: nonlinear regression and Excel solver
In-Reply-To: <000d01c3daca$1c36fac0$8f5a3f92@labor.ak>
References: <000d01c3daca$1c36fac0$8f5a3f92@labor.ak>
Message-ID: <40058FF0.8080606@pdf.com>

     Thanks, Nels, for your excellent commentary.  I've found Excel 
useful for one more thing in this regard:  I often program the same 
thing in Excel and S-Plus or R and use each to check the other.  I've 
had reasonable luck getting sensible answers from Excel IF I GAVE IT 
REASONABLE STARTING VALUES.  If I can't ultimately get the same answer 
from Excel and S-Plus / R, I must have an error in at least one of them. 

     I recently used optim for a rather difficult numerical 
optimization problem.  I compared its 4 methods, Nelder-Mead, BFGS, CG, 
and simulated annealing (SANN).  In many cases, I was able to get better 
answers by using the output of one optim run as starting values for a 
second attempt at the same problem.  None of the methods was uniformly 
better than the other three, but SANN was best in only one of roughly 50 
comparisons I ran, and usually the worst or close to the worst;  in 
addition, SANN took roughly 5 to 10 times as long to get an answer.  
Nelder-Mead was usually the fastest, but CG more often produced the best 
answer (i.e., found a solution with a lower minimum for the objective 
function).  The problem I used is not simple enough to describe easily, 
and it was poorly parameterized, which was part of the problem.  My 
conclusions from this are to consider alternative parameterizations and 
try different things. 

     hope this helps. 
     Spencer Graves

