Power analysis tells you the probability that you would obtain a dataset
that would give you evidence of a certain effect when the effect is of a
hypothesized size.  Why would you want to figure out that probability when
you already have the dataset (and can summarize what it tells you about
the parameters using confidence intervals, credible intervals, or whatever
your philosophy prefers)?

See for example:
Hoenig, John M. and Heisey, Dennis M. (2001) 
The abuse of power: The pervasive fallacy of power calculations for data
analysis 
The American Statistician, 55, 19-24 

