To all:

As always, I appreciate Alan's and Spencer's thoughful comments. I find them
always educational and often entertaining (which goes to show, I suppose,
how truly nerdy I am).

May I just add a brief addendum to Alan's exceptional comment, which I'm
saving and passing on. He notes:

>> However I calculate a 95%
>> confidence (or credible) interval of (-1,+3) and conclude that the data
>> are consistent at conventional levels with my previously hypothesized
>> effect of +2 and even larger ones. 

True, of course. But subtly implied here is a 0-1 loss function: values
within the interval are equally supported by the data, while those without
are not. Of course, we know this isn't true. Values nearer to 1 are much
more strongly supported by the data than are values near -1 or 3. How much
more? ... Well, that's why we are all at least closet Bayesians, n'est-ce
pas? Alan, I believe, is quite open in his advocacy of the Bayesian
paradigm, so he is beyond reproach on this matter. But frequentist
phraseology can be a trap,

Cheers to all. Keep the questions and comments coming. Pearls like this
discussion are a delight.

