Cc: Stiff, Charles (TIFPC); s-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu
Subject: Re: [S] Power and mixed effects models

Before an experiment, I might conjecture based on prior information that
the standard error of my estimated effect will be 1, so the estimated
effect would have to be about 2 to be significantly different from 0. If I
hypothesize that the actual effect is about 2, then there is a 50% chance
of an effect >2 (showing significance) and 50% of <2 (not significant).
This is a power calculation, and would help me in the decision whether to
increase the size of the planned experiment or indeed not to let it go
forward at all.

After the experiment, I might observe an effect estimate of 1, which is
not significantly different from the null of 0.  However I calculate a 95%
confidence (or credible) interval of (-1,+3) and conclude that the data
are consistent at conventional levels with my previously hypothesized
effect of +2 and even larger ones.  I conclude that the data are not
adequate to either disprove the null or exclude my original hypothesis of
some interesting effect size.  (One might say that the experiment was a
inconclusive, although of course it generates information that in the
longer run of science might be useful.)  However, in this calculation I
only consider intervals around (inferences from) the estimated effect that
I observed, not the distribution of possible effect sizes that I might
have observed.  Hence this is a different calculation from the one in the
power analysis, which is no longer relevant.

On Thu, 15 Jan 2004, Spencer Graves wrote:
>       I'm confused.  If my data analysis is not statistically 
> significant, it does NOT mean that there was nothing there, only that I 
> didn't find it. 

