f1(x) = f(X1, X2, ..., Xn)
f2(x) = g(X1, X2, ..., Xn)

Standard optimization techniques could be used to find an optimum for f1(x) or 
f2(x) 
but an optimum that satisfies both f1 and f2 is mainly a compromise. 
Compromising 
will be even more with more functions than two and with different feasible 
regions for 
the functions. A Pareto-Optimal set or front is all the solutions that are not 
dominated 
or those that are best in at least one aspect.

An application might be f1 is quality of materials used in a product and f2 is 
cost. So 
one would need to maximize f1 and minimize f2. If f1 is on y-axis and f2 on 
x-axis, 
you would like to select among those solutions that are on the upper left front.

Thank you
 

