Message-ID: <4008721D.5000705@pburns.seanet.com>

Perhaps someone has a more clever method, but one approach
is to perform an optimization on linear combinations of the functions.
In your example of maximizing f1 and minimizing f2 the objective
would be

 h(x) = a * (-f1(x)) + (1 - a) * f2(x)

Optimizations would be performed on h for a range of choices of a.

When there are more than two functions, you would have a vector
of coefficients for the functions that sums to 1.  This could lead to a
large number of optimizations if the grid over the allowable coefficients
is very fine.  You can use a nearby solution as a starting point, however,
to help speed the computation.

For specifics in S, you can use "nlminb" in S-PLUS, and "optim" in R.
If your functions are particularly nasty, you could try "genopt" from S
Poetry.

